# A1 — Governed Agent Execution Interface

Status: **A1 COMPLETE; READY FOR A2 IMPLEMENTATION (separate authorization required)**

This Forge control-plane architecture addresses AQ3.2. It changes no product repository and authorizes no implementation dispatch.

## Authority and roles

The Architect owns the immutable work envelope, baseline, repositories, paths, frozen components, acceptance criteria, capability grants, and escalation decisions. The Agent owns reasoning and requests; it has no filesystem, process, network, registry, scope, or policy authority. The Governed Execution Interface (GEI) owns authorization, canonicalization, effect mediation, execution scopes, revocation, quiescence, provenance, and audit. Policy and audit storage are inaccessible to the Agent.

The default is deny. `unmediated_effect_capable_agent_tools = NONE` is an invariant: raw host shell, patch, stdin, MCP, plugin, browser, and connector tools are not presented to the reasoning session. Requests arriving through any other path are denied at the host boundary.

## Typed contract

```text
WorkAuthorization { id, revision, work_package, baseline, session_id, turn_id,
  repositories, grants, protected_paths, frozen_components, command_classes,
  environment_allowlist, network_policy, external_policy, limits,
  state: ACTIVE|REVOKED|EXPIRED }
GovernedInvocation { id, authorization_id, session_id, turn_id, scope_id,
  tool, normalized_request, decision, started, ended, result, diagnostics }
AuthorityExpansionRequest { id, authorization_id, session_id, turn_id,
  capability, intended_action, resources, reason, consequence }
ExecutionScopeStatus { scope_id, authorization_id, session_id, turn_id,
  supervisor, state: ACTIVE|REVOKING|QUIESCENT|REVOCATION_FAILED,
  active_members, revoke_at, quiescent_at }
ArchitecturalTurnStatus { codex_state, authorization_state, scope_state,
  pending_invocations, state: AUTHORIZED|RUNNING|ESCALATION_PENDING|
  INTERRUPTING|QUIESCENT|COMPLETE|FAILED_REVOCATION }
```

Authorization revisions are immutable. A replacement creates a new revision and does not silently broaden an in-flight turn. Deny overrides every grant; ambiguous canonicalization, stale baseline, missing context, or policy failure denies.

## V1 governed tools

`governed_read`, `governed_list`, and `governed_search` canonicalize paths, reject traversal and disallowed symlinks, enforce read grants, bound output, and log hashes and provenance. `governed_write` permits only authorized files, validates before mutation, uses atomic replacement where feasible, and treats delete, rename, mode, hard-link, and symlink operations as explicit grants. `governed_patch` preflights every touched path and rejects the entire patch if any path is denied.

`governed_exec` accepts normalized argv, authorized executable/class and cwd, bounded environment, timeout, and output limits. Direct argv is the default; shell syntax is a separate grant. Every process enters the single turn execution scope owned by GEI. `governed_status` exposes non-secret authorization, invocation, scope, escalation, and architectural state.

Git inspection is read-only by default. Git mutation, package installation, system modification, credentials, persistent services, and network are separate explicit grants and denied by default.

External tools are a separate capability plane. Catalog visibility never grants invocation. The registry is generated by GEI from authorization and immutable to the Agent; host denial remains mandatory.

## Execution, revocation, and status

GEI creates one turn-level scope before presenting the first effect-capable tool. The AQ3.1 PID-namespace supervisor is the selected containment mechanism: external supervisor, bubblewrap namespace, `die-with-parent`, and independent scope-empty observation. Each invocation binds authorization, session, turn, invocation, and scope IDs. Detached, reparented, `setsid`, double-fork, stubborn, and background descendants remain namespace members. The Agent cannot create or mark a scope quiescent.

Interruption closes admission, marks REVOKING, terminates cooperatively, escalates SIGTERM then bounded SIGKILL/namespace teardown, verifies zero effect-capable members and no later effects, and only then marks QUIESCENT. Failure is REVOCATION_FAILED and never clean idle. Architectural status is Codex terminal AND scope QUIESCENT AND no pending invocation. The same gate applies to ordinary completion. A fresh turn receives a fresh scope ID.

Invocation lifecycle: REQUESTED → AUTHORIZED → EXECUTING → SUCCEEDED|FAILED|DENIED|CANCELLED, with REVOKING where applicable. Audit is GEI-written and records normalized request, decision, timing, scope, bounded result, and evidence without unnecessary sensitive contents.

## Escalation and controls

An out-of-envelope request yields `AuthorityExpansionRequest`; no effect starts. The Architect may approve narrowly, reject, replace authorization, or terminate the turn. Approval binds exact session, turn, authorization revision, capability, and normalized request. There is no self-approval, generic allow-all, or cross-turn replay.

| Threat | Control |
|---|---|
| Outside read/write and path escape | canonical path authority, deny precedence, preflight |
| Partial patch mutation | whole-patch validation before write |
| Rogue or detached process | one supervisor-owned PID namespace; scope-empty oracle |
| Interrupt race or delayed effect | admission gate, revoke, quiescence-before-status |
| Raw tool bypass | GEI-owned registry; built-ins disabled; host deny |
| Network/plugin/credential/system effect | separate grants, default deny |
| Destructive Git/package install | distinct explicit grants |
| Self-expansion/replay | immutable revisions and bound approvals |
| Audit tampering | GEI-owned append-only evidence outside Agent scope |
| Controller/broker failure | watchdog/parent-death policy; otherwise FAILED_REVOCATION |

## Host architecture decision

Candidate A, a custom Codex host with a GEI registry, is selected because it owns tool presentation and can remove raw built-ins without maintaining a large model fork. MCP-only governance is rejected because current host tools can coexist and bypass it. An external reasoning orchestrator is a fallback with more relay/state complexity. A Codex fork has excessive maintenance burden. Manual execution does not test the intended autonomous loop.

Selected A2 architecture: a small custom Codex host/adapter runs Codex as a reasoning engine with no effect-capable built-ins, presents only GEI tools, and routes `governed_exec` to the AQ3.1 supervisor. The host owns session/turn mapping; GEI owns authorization and scope lifecycle; the Architect owns work authorization. Image/plugin/catalog activity remains EXTERNAL_CAPABILITY_PLANE for AQ4.

## A2 boundary

A2 may implement only the host adapter/GEI and scratch fake repositories. It must demonstrate registry exclusion, typed path policy, governed patch/exec, scope integration, revocation/quiescence gating, approval denial, independent audit, and repeat AQ1–AQ3 controls including detached/stubborn workers, delayed/open-descriptor effects, completion leaks, failure handling, reuse, and no-bypass audit. No E1 repository is used until qualification passes.

E1-WP-001 remains compatible: its work envelope is unchanged; only its future adapter changes to GEI. A1 makes no production changes and issues no implementation work.
